  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 105 PM CDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\nWith the upper-level trough pushing into the Texas panhandle \nand associated upper-level disturbances tracking across the \nregion Wednesday night through Friday morning, we will see the \nweather pattern switch from dry and warm to wet and cool for the \nsecond half of this week. Given this setup with persistent \nsoutherly flow transporting ample amounts of moisture to the \nregion, models continue to show higher PW (precipitable water)\nvalues of 1.5 to 2 inches during this timeframe. This is why much\nof West Central Texas is currently outlooked for a Marginal to\nSlight Risk for Excessive Rainfall by WPC for Wednesday through \nFriday morning. \n\nThe potential for heavy rainfall and localized flash flooding \nacross the area has increased with the latest model trends, but we \nwill have to watch this closely as the finer details will play a \nrole on the where the heaviest rainfall bands set up. Also, there \nis a low chance of an isolated marginal severe storm or two during\nthis period. Please keep up with the latest weather information. \nThe Pops will be medium to high chances during this event. We \nbegin to dry out again by this weekend, as this system weakens \nand high pressure build back behind it. As for temperatures,\ncooler high temperatures in the lower 60s to lower 70s are\nexpected for Friday and through much of the weekend. A cold front\nwill track through the area Friday, bringing breezy and cooler \nconditions in its wake.",
